Question 4
----------- In vs Exists vs Join
In
-- :- In checks whether a value matches any value in a list or the result of a subquery.
Think of IN as asking "Is this value in the list?"

SELECT CustomerID, Name     -- Can return multiple columns
FROM Customers
WHERE CustomerID IN (
    SELECT CustomerID       -- Must return ONE column for this IN comparison
    FROM Orders
);

What If Duplicates exists?
--------------------------
DId  | Dname
10   | IT
10   | Information technology
20   | HR

IN:- Ravi(Customer) appears once because his department ID matches the list.

EXISTS
------
Exists checks whether a subquery retunrs at least one row that satisfies its condition.
Think of IN as asking "Is there at least one matching row?"

select c.CustomerID,c.CustName
from Customers c 
where not exists (select 1
                  from orders o
                  where o.CustomerID = c.CustomerID)

What If Duplicates exists?
--------------------------
Exists :- Ravi (Customer) appears once because SQL only checks whether a matching row exists.

Joins:
-----
Join combines rows from two tables based on a matching condition. you can retrive columns from both tables.
Think of IN as asking "Combines the matching rows from these tables"
What If Duplicates exists?
--------------------------
Join:- Ravi appears twice beacuse his department ID matches two department rows.

Performance Tip:- don't assume Exists is always faster than IN na d JOIN. doern databse optimizes can produce similar execution plans . 
performance depends on the database, indexes, data distribution, and query structure.

